# §4.44 Other Applications
%%%
number := false
%%%

This page is an applications overview and contains no numbered mathematical
displays. It records where elementary combinations of exponentials and
logarithms enter applied models, and points to the relevant references and
neighboring DLMF sections.

::::dlmfEntry "Applications and editorial addition" "https://dlmf.nist.gov/4.44#info"
The current DLMF version notes that the paragraph on the Einstein functions
and Planck's radiation function was added effective with version 1.0.3. The
addition also records the associated references and adds a reference to
Corless et al. in the final paragraph.
::::

::::dlmfEntry "Einstein functions and Planck radiation" "https://dlmf.nist.gov/4.44#p1"
The Einstein functions and Planck's radiation function are elementary
combinations of exponentials, or of exponentials and logarithms. DLMF points
to Abramowitz and Stegun (1964, pp. 999–1000) and Ashcroft and Mermin (1976,
Chapter 23).
::::

::::dlmfEntry "Generalized exponentials and logarithms" "https://dlmf.nist.gov/4.44#p2"
Applications of generalized exponentials and generalized logarithms to
computer arithmetic are referred to DLMF §3.1(iv). Applications of the
Lambert W-function to generalized Gaussian noise are referenced to
Chapeau-Blondeau and Monir (2002).
::::

::::dlmfEntry "Further Lambert W applications" "https://dlmf.nist.gov/4.44#p3"
For other applications of the Lambert W-function, DLMF refers to Corless et
al. (1996). The page links forward to §4.45, Methods of Computation, and back
to §4.43, Cubic Equations.
::::
